<Base title="Indexes" description="Follow an ordered index from its search tree to table rows, then choose and check an index for filtering, ordering and the cost of keeping it current.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design schema-sketches">
  <header class="essay-header">
   <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
   <h1>Indexes</h1>
   <div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a><a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Next: How a query runs →</a></div>
  </header>
  <p>An <strong>index</strong> helps a database find rows without searching the whole table. It stores values from selected columns in a structure that supports fast lookups. In PostgreSQL, it also stores references to the table rows those values came from.</p>
  <p>Consider finding one customer’s orders in a table containing orders for thousands of customers. Without a suitable index, the database may have to check every row to find the ones belonging to that customer. An index ordered by customer ID groups the customer’s entries together and points to their orders, even when those orders are scattered through the table. Once it finds the first matching entry, the database can read the following matching entries.</p>
  <p>The index’s arrangement determines what else it can do. An index on customer ID finds a customer’s orders, but it does not put them in date order. Adding the date to the index can also help a query return the newest orders first. These improvements have a cost: indexes take up storage, and the database must keep them up to date as orders are added or changed.</p>

  <nav class="index-contents" aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#route">B-tree indexes</a></li><li><a href="#history">Composite indexes</a></li><li><a href="#remaining">Filtering</a></li><li><a href="#covering">Covering indexes</a></li><li><a href="#cost">Storage and maintenance</a></li><li><a href="#check">Testing an index</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="route">
   <h2>B-tree indexes</h2>
   <p>PostgreSQL 18 uses a <strong>B-tree</strong> by default when you create an index. In an index on customer ID, the customer IDs are its <strong>keys</strong>, the values it organises for searching. A B-tree keeps these keys in order and uses a hierarchy of pages to locate the first matching entry.</p>
   <p>The database stores information in fixed-size chunks called <strong>pages</strong>. At the top of the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html#BTREE-STRUCTURE">tree</a>, the root page divides keys into ranges and points towards smaller parts of the index. Each step excludes ranges that cannot contain the requested key. Eventually the search reaches a <strong>leaf page</strong>, where the entries point to table rows.</p>
   <IndexRoute />
   <p>The root and other branching pages can each point to many child pages, so the tree can hold many more orders without becoming much deeper. A search reads the pages leading to the first match, then follows the matching leaf entries. To find a range of values, PostgreSQL starts at one boundary and reads along the leaves until it reaches the other.</p>
   <p>Finding an entry is only part of retrieving an order. In PostgreSQL, an ordinary index scan follows its reference into the table’s main storage, called the <strong>heap</strong>. That is where it can get fields the index does not contain and check that the row version belongs in this query’s view of the database. Nearby index entries can point to distant table pages, so finding several entries together does not mean PostgreSQL can fetch all their rows from one page.</p>
   <p>The visibility check matters when requests overlap. PostgreSQL can retain an earlier row version for a reader whose view predates an update, while newer requests see the changed version. An index entry locates a candidate; the engine must still check whether that version belongs in this query’s view. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">storage article</a> follows how those versions are created and eventually removed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="history">
   <h2>Composite indexes</h2>
   <p>Let’s put the order-history request into a running database. You are the developer, sending SQL from a query editor or <code>psql</code> to a PostgreSQL 18 session. Run the setup below once in a fresh session, then keep that session open as we change the indexes. It creates a temporary table that disappears on disconnect. All the commands in this article act on that table. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/practice/">practice guide</a> explains how to open a PostgreSQL database; use the separate setup here for this investigation.</p>
   <p>We generate 60,000 invented orders for 1,000 customers. Each customer has 60 orders, spread through time. Order IDs increase by one each minute from 1 January; customers repeat in a cycle. Customer 42 has 15 orders on or after 1 February. Each row also has a long delivery note, making it much larger than an index entry containing just a customer ID and a row location. We use regular, invented data so we can check the counts as we go.</p>
   <Sql code={setupText} label="Create and populate the isolated temporary orders table" />
   <p><code>generate_series</code> supplies one row for each integer <code>n</code> from 1 to 60,000. The remainder operator <code>%</code> cycles through customer IDs, multiplying the one-minute interval advances the time, and <code>CASE</code> chooses a status. These expressions manufacture a repeatable dataset; they are not part of the history query we want to improve.</p>
   <p><code>ANALYZE</code> collects statistics that help PostgreSQL estimate how many rows a condition will match. The primary key already creates an index on <code>order_id</code>. That index is useful when we know an order ID. Our history request knows a customer and a date instead:</p>
   <Sql code={history} label="Ask for customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February" />
   <p><code>WHERE</code> specifies the customer and earliest date. <code>ORDER BY</code> asks for newest first, with descending order ID breaking any timestamp ties. <code>LIMIT 10</code> keeps the first ten of that ordered result. The query returns order IDs 59041, 58041, 57041, 56041, 55041, 54041, 53041, 52041, 51041 and 50041. Each index we try should return those same ten orders.</p>
   <p>Start by giving the database the customer lookup we just pictured:</p>
   <Sql code={customerIndex} label="Create an index with customer ID as its key" />
   <p>Now the engine can identify the customer’s 60 orders without inspecting every other customer. But the index has no date key. It still has to fetch those orders, reject the 45 before February, and sort the remaining 15 to find the newest ten. An index on the date as well would let us skip those older orders.</p>
   <p>A <strong>composite index</strong> has more than one key column. Its column order defines how entries are arranged: compare the first column, use the next to order entries tied on the first, and continue if those values tie too. For this screen, put the customer first and dates newest first within each customer:</p>
   <Sql code={compositeIndex} label="Replace the customer index with an ordered composite index" />
   <CompositeOrder />
   <p>The equality condition selects customer 42’s run. Within that run the dates are ordered, so the February boundary cuts off the older entries. Starting from the newest end also produces exactly the requested order. Once ten qualifying rows have been returned, the executor can stop. It does not need to collect and sort all 15.</p>
   <p>The final <code>order_id DESC</code> makes the index order agree with the complete SQL order, including ties. These examples contain no tied timestamps, but actual order histories can. An index on just customer and date does not itself supply the requested ordering between orders placed at the same instant. Keep <code>ORDER BY</code> in the query even when an index can provide it: SQL does not promise result order without it.</p>
   <p>Would putting the date first do the same thing? It would group all customers’ newest orders together. That could help a screen showing the newest orders across the shop, but finding ten for one customer could require passing many other customers’ entries. Choose the column order for the query: which entries should be together, which should come first, and when can the scan stop?</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL can scan a B-tree backwards as well as forwards. An all-ascending index on these same three keys could serve this query by scanning customer 42’s run backwards. Mixed ordering needs more care: scanning backwards reverses every key direction together. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-ordering.html">ordering documentation</a> develops that distinction.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="remaining">
   <h2>Filtering</h2>
   <p>Suppose the history screen now asks for paid orders only. Add one condition to the same request:</p>
   <Sql code={residual} label="Return the newest paid orders for customer 42" />
   <p>PostgreSQL can still find customer 42’s recent orders in the index. It then has to fetch them from the table to check <code>status</code>, which the index does not contain. In our data, the scan encounters two pending orders before finding ten paid ones. It returns ten rows after examining twelve candidates. With a customer whose recent orders were mostly pending, it could inspect much more of the run.</p>
   <p>The status check is a <strong>filter</strong> applied after finding the rows through the index. In PostgreSQL’s execution plan, <code>Index Cond</code> shows conditions checked by the index scan, while <code>Filter</code> shows a separate check. Even an <code>Index Cond</code> does not always reduce how much of the index is scanned: a condition on a later key column may reject entries along the way.</p>
   <p>If the shop often needs paid-only histories, we could try <code>(customer_id, status, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code>. The two equality conditions select one customer’s paid run before the date range. For the original history screen, however, a customer’s orders are now grouped by status, so dates across all statuses no longer form one uninterrupted sequence.</p>
   <p>Adding <code>status</code> helps one query and changes what the index can do for another. The column order matters as much as which columns are present. A missing leading condition does not make a PostgreSQL index unusable, either: PostgreSQL 18 can sometimes make repeated searches over distinct leading values, called a <strong>skip scan</strong>. It is most useful when there are few such values. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">multicolumn documentation</a> explains when these searches can help. Check the execution plan to see what PostgreSQL chooses for your data.</p>
   <p>There is another way to specialise a frequent query. A <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-partial.html"><strong>partial index</strong></a> contains only rows satisfying its own condition. An index of pending orders could be small if almost every order is already complete. PostgreSQL can use that index when it knows the query asks only for pending orders. If status comes from a parameter, PostgreSQL may build a plan to reuse with different parameter values. That plan must also work for other statuses, which the pending-only index cannot supply. For our general history screen, we will keep the full index.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="covering">
   <h2>Covering indexes</h2>
   <p>Return to the original history query, which includes pending and paid orders. Our composite index contains customer ID, timestamp and order ID. The result also asks for <code>total</code>. Each of its ten rows therefore needs a table fetch to obtain that amount.</p>
   <p>We can add <code>total</code> to the index with PostgreSQL’s <code>INCLUDE</code> clause. It stores the value alongside each entry without making it part of the sort key.</p>
   <Sql code={coveringIndex} label="Replace the composite index with one that also carries the amount" />
   <p>This index <strong>covers</strong> the query: all the values it needs are available there. An <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html"><strong>index-only scan</strong></a> can return those values. PostgreSQL still has to establish that each row is visible to the transaction. It keeps a small visibility map recording table pages whose rows are visible to all transactions. When a page has that mark, the scan can avoid fetching it; otherwise it must visit the heap to check.</p>
   <p>Before vacuuming, our covering plan reported <code>Heap Fetches: 10</code>: it still fetched all ten rows from the table. Run this command on its own, with autocommit enabled:</p>
   <Sql code="VACUUM (ANALYZE) index_orders;" label="Reclaim eligible versions and update page visibility information" />
   <p>Vacuum marked eligible pages as visible to all transactions. The same history query then reported <code>Heap Fetches: 0</code>. Vacuum changed the table’s visibility information, so PostgreSQL could now get the same answer without visiting the table. The final section shows how to collect these plan counters at each step. A busy table with recent changes may offer fewer opportunities to avoid those visits.</p>
   <p>The covering index can also help with a query that reads many more rows. Consider summing the amounts for 900 of our 1,000 customers:</p>
   <Sql code={broad} label="Sum order totals for most of the customers" />
   <p>That condition matches 54,000 orders. With an index that requires table fetches, visiting nearly the whole table through individual references can be more work than simply scanning it. With our vacuumed covering index, PostgreSQL chose an index-only scan instead: the smaller index supplied all the amounts.</p>
   <p><strong>Selectivity</strong> describes the fraction of rows a condition selects. A condition matching very few rows is usually called selective. The planner also considers how wide the rows are, how they are spread over pages, expected caching, and whether the query can stop early. Depending on those factors, it may choose a table scan or read much of a smaller covering index. The fraction of matching rows is useful information, but cannot by itself tell us which will do less work.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost">
   <h2>Storage and maintenance</h2>
   <p>Every insert or update may also require changes to the indexes. Inserting an order adds a table row and entries in its indexes. If a B-tree leaf page has no room, PostgreSQL can <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html#BTREE-IMPLEMENTATION">split it</a> and change the parent page to point to the new pages. Changing a key value requires an entry at its new position. Updating an included value such as <code>total</code> also requires index maintenance, even though it is not a search key.</p>
   <p>Deleted rows and superseded versions can leave entries that become removable later. PostgreSQL removes these entries through mechanisms including <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html">vacuum</a>. These changes and the later cleanup use CPU, memory and I/O in addition to the index’s storage space.</p>
   <p>In our generated example, the table occupied about 13.5 MiB and the covering index about 2.9 MiB in the test runtime, excluding the separate primary-key index. The extra amount column is only a few bytes per entry here. Including a long delivery note would be a different decision: it would copy much more data into an index used to answer a small history screen. Index pages also compete with useful table pages for cache space.</p>
   <p>A primary key or unique constraint has a second reason to keep its supporting index: enforcing the rule. Check that purpose before treating an infrequently read index as redundant. Likewise, a new composite index beginning with <code>customer_id</code> may make a separate customer-only index unnecessary for this workload, but its smaller size can still matter for other queries. Check the other queries that use an index before removing it.</p>
   <p>Creating the index is work too. It must read existing data and build the structure. Our ordinary <code>CREATE INDEX</code> commands are appropriate for an isolated temporary table. On a live PostgreSQL table, an ordinary build blocks writes while it runs. A concurrent build changes that behaviour and adds its own costs and failure handling; the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY">concurrent-build documentation</a> is the next step before planning that operation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
   <h2>Testing an index</h2>
   <p class="example-note">The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-order">index-order lesson</a> runs this article’s customer history experiment. Replace the customer-only index, compare the same ten results, then put status before time and remove the status filter to see what changes. For a shorter introduction, try the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-selectivity">index-selectivity lesson</a> with 20,000 generated orders. Compare the same customer lookup before and after creating an index, then widen the filter and inspect the planner’s choice. The browser exercise measures no production workload.</p>
   <p>We expect the customer index to skip other customers’ orders. The composite index should also remove the sort and let the scan stop after ten matches. The covering index may avoid table fetches. To check, run the history query with <code>EXPLAIN</code>:</p>
   <Sql code={check} label="Execute the history query and inspect its work" />
   <p><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> executes the query and reports what its plan nodes actually did. <code>BUFFERS</code> adds page-access counters; <code>TIMING OFF</code> avoids measuring each node’s elapsed time while retaining row counts and total execution time. The word <code>ANALYZE</code> here means “run and observe”; the standalone <code>ANALYZE index_orders</code> command earlier collects planner statistics.</p>
   <p>The <a href={experimentUrl} download="index-experiment.sql">complete experiment</a> runs the setup and each replacement in order, with a plan after each step. It also exercises inserts, amount changes and deletes. Run the file with <code>psql -f index-experiment.sql</code> against your practice database, or execute its statements individually in a query editor with autocommit enabled. <code>VACUUM</code> cannot run inside a transaction block. Use a fresh session; running the setup twice in the same session will find the temporary table already there. Our verification used PGlite 0.5.8, an embedded WebAssembly build reporting PostgreSQL 18.3. These are observed row counts in that setting, not server latency measurements:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed work for the same history query"><table><thead><tr><th>Available history index</th><th>Observed work</th><th>Separate sort?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>None</td><td>Table scan: 15 rows passed, 59,985 rejected</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Customer only</td><td>60 candidates; 45 rejected by the date filter</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Customer, date, order</td><td>Index scan returned 10 rows, then stopped</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Same keys, including total</td><td>10 rows; heap fetches fell from 10 to 0 after vacuum</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>For the customer-only case, PostgreSQL chose a <strong>bitmap scan</strong>: collect matching row locations, then visit the table pages holding them. That improves the organisation of table access, but does not preserve the index’s ordering. It still needed the date filter and sort. PostgreSQL can also combine results from multiple indexes this way; two single-column indexes do not automatically supply the ordered run of one composite index. See <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-bitmap-scans.html">combining indexes</a>.</p>
   <p>The essential part of the composite plan looked like this, with costs, buffer counts and PostgreSQL’s explicit type casts omitted:</p>
   <pre class="plan"><code>{narrowPlan}</code></pre>
   <p>Read it from the indented scan towards its parent. The scan supplies rows in order; <code>Limit</code> stops requesting them after ten. <code>actual rows=10</code> means ten rows emitted by that node, not ten total operations inside the tree. It does not count all key comparisons or page visits. Here each node ran once. In more complex plans, row counts are per loop and need to be read with <code>loops</code>.</p>
   <p>So far we have read from a fixed set of orders. The downloadable experiment also inserts 1,000 orders, changes 200 amounts and deletes 100 orders:</p>
   <Sql code={writes} label="Exercise inserts, included-column updates and deletes in the temporary table" />
   <p>The script checks that the table contains 60,900 rows and that all 200 corrected orders have a total of 26.00. It then reports table and index sizes and shows the history query’s plan again. To measure the cost of maintaining the index, we need a different test: temporary tables have different storage and logging behaviour, and PGlite does not reproduce a networked server’s workload.</p>
   <p>Before choosing an index for the application, repeat the comparison on a disposable test database with ordinary durable tables. Use realistic row sizes, customers with different numbers of orders, and the application’s prepared queries. Run the same read and write mix against the current indexes and each candidate. Compare query latency and throughput, including slower requests, alongside changed plans, buffer work, index size and write latency. Let the run include sustained updates and maintenance; a freshly built index on an otherwise idle database misses part of the cost.</p>
   <p>For this history screen, the composite index lets PostgreSQL find one customer’s recent orders and stop after ten, without a separate sort. Including <code>total</code> can save the remaining table fetches, but makes the index larger and adds work when totals change. The read and write measurements will help us decide whether that saving is worth it.</p>
  </section>

